 <office:body>
  <office:text>
   <text:sequence-decls><text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Figure"/></text:sequence-decls>
   <text:p text:style-name="Title">A short guide to the kitchen garden</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">A kitchen garden is a small plot, close to the house, where vegetables and herbs are grown for the table. It does not need much space: a few raised beds in a sunny corner are enough for a family. The soil should be <text:span text:style-name="Strong">loose and rich</text:span>, so it is worth adding compost every autumn and letting the worms do the rest over the winter. See <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="category-and-value" text:ref-name="refFigure0">Figure 1</text:sequence-ref>, written on <text:date style:data-style-name="N37" text:date-value="2026-10-22">October 22, 2026</text:date>, under <text:bookmark-ref text:reference-format="text" text:ref-name="plant">What to plant first</text:bookmark-ref>.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="plant"/>What to plant first<text:bookmark-end text:name="plant"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Beginners do well with lettuce, radishes and beans. They grow quickly, forgive the odd mistake, and give a harvest within a few weeks of sowing. Tomatoes and peppers are more demanding: they want warmth, regular water and a stake to lean on, but a single plant of each can carry fruit all summer long.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Herbs belong near the kitchen door. Parsley, chives and thyme are picked a few leaves at a time, and a cook who has to cross the garden for them will soon stop bothering. Mint spreads without limit, so keep it in a pot.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2">Water and weeds</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Water in the morning, at the roots rather than over the leaves, and water deeply but not every day. Plants that are watered a little and often grow shallow roots and wilt in the first hot week. Weeds are easiest to pull when they are small and the ground is damp, so a few minutes after each rain saves hours later in the season.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure0" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">1</text:sequence>: Keep a notebook. Write down what was sown where and when it came up, what did well and what the slugs ate. The second year is always better than the first, mostly because of the notes.</text:p>
  </office:text>
 </office:body>
</office:document>
